            "kind": "NO_CODE_CHANGE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "Can you try changing the declaration order of:\n\nhttps://github.com/openbmc/bmcweb/blob/d1a3caa4eda8ca07cd656b4e4e8aa1e4ffe53e1e/redfish-core/include/subscription.hpp#L105C1-L106C1\n\nclient and hbtimer here\n\nAnd see if the error codes away?  client should probably be the last thing declared."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Myung Bae",
                        "email": "myungbae@us.ibm.com",
                        "username": "baemyung"
                    },
                    "message": "The variables are defined in this order, \n```\n    std::optional<crow::HttpClient> client;\n    boost::asio::steady_timer hbTimer;\n```\n\nSo, wouldn't the destruction order be \n(1) hbTimer is destroyed first\n(2) and then client is destroyed next?\n\nAssuming if the order is changed (i.e. client destruction first before hbTimer), will deleteSubscription()[by API] clear up any async_io waits?\n\nWould the race between the manual deleteSubscription (via Redfish API) and async_io callback may still happen in either order?\n\nAnyway, as suggested, let's change the variable definition order -- but I still think the shared_from_this() to hold object would still be needed."
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ed Tanous",
                        "email": "ed@tanous.net",
                        "username": "edtanous"
                    },
                    "message": "> The variables are defined in this order, \n> ```\n>     std::optional<crow::HttpClient> client;\n>     boost::asio::steady_timer hbTimer;\n> ```\n> \n> So, wouldn't the destruction order be \n> (1) hbTimer is destroyed first\n> (2) and then client is destroyed next?\n> \n\nCorrect.\n\n> Assuming if the order is changed (i.e. client destruction first before hbTimer), will deleteSubscription()[by API] clear up any async_io waits?\n\nThe bug I suspect is that the heartbeat timer might still hold a reference while this is being destroyed, and the callback doesn't handle it well.  IT's a guess.\n\n> \n> Would the race between the manual deleteSubscription (via Redfish API) and async_io callback may still happen in either order?\n\nAll async operations are canceled on destruction of the object.\n\n> \n> Anyway, as suggested, let's change the variable definition order -- but I still think the shared_from_this() to hold object would still be needed."
                }
            ],
